<spell-notebook "item theming">
	<header level="1">
		Item
		<header>One item for dropdowns, lists and menus, rendered by whatever holds it.</>
	</>
	<item-tab-bar showing="theming"/>
	<section>
		<menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
			<item link="#tokens">Tokens</>
			<item link="#themes">Themes</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- THEMING:  the family's tokens, live, plus how to set them and which themes touch it. -->
	<section label="Tokens">
		<markdown>
			An item has no tokens of its own: its look comes from its owner, so theme it through the list, menu, dropdown or
			Items-view tokens (their Theming tabs). `UIItem.css` declares only a private flag (`--_ui-item-media`); never set
			it.

			Style the pieces of a list or menu item with `::part()`: `item` (the box), `icon` and `image`.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```css
			ui-menu ui-item::part(item) {
			  font-weight: bold;
			}
			ui-list ui-item::part(image) {
			  border-radius: 0.25em;
			}
			```
		</>
		<html>
			<ui-docs-tokens family="ui-item" playground></>
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Themes">
		<markdown>
			No theme restyles items directly: a theme that restyles lists, menus or dropdowns restyles their items. The theme
			picker in the header applies a theme to the whole site.
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
